🌟 Kubernetes – Simple Notes
🚀 1. What is Kubernetes? (Intro)
Kubernetes (K8s) is an open‑source container orchestration platform that automatically manages:
Deployment
Scaling
Load balancing
Self-healing
Updates of containerized applications

Created by Google, now maintained by CNCF.
👉 Think of Kubernetes as the “operating system” for containerized apps running in a cluster.

❓ 2. Why Kubernetes? (Why do we use it?)
Kubernetes solves real problems in modern cloud apps:
Scalability
Automatically increases/decreases number of containers based on load.

High Availability
Restarts failed containers → ensures app stays up.

Load Balancing
Distributes traffic to healthy containers.

Rolling Updates
Deploy new versions without downtime.

Resource Optimization
Efficiently uses CPU/RAM across nodes.

Portable
Runs on AWS, Azure, GCP, On‑prem, anywhere.
👉 Main Purpose: Run containers at scale automatically and reliably.

🏗️ 3. Kubernetes Architecture (Objects & Their Role)
Kubernetes has a Master (Control Plane) and Worker Nodes.
⭐ Control Plane Components (Brain of K8s)
1. APIServer
Entry point for all K8s commands (kubectl).
Validates and processes requests.

2. etcd
Key‑value database.
Stores entire cluster state.

3. Scheduler
Decides which node a pod should run on.

4. Controller Manager
Ensures the actual state matches desired state.
Manages:
ReplicaSets
Node health
Endpoints
Jobs etc.




🖥️ Worker Node Components (Where Apps Run)
1. Kubelet
Agent on each node.
Starts/stops containers.

2. Kube-Proxy
Manages networking & load balancing inside cluster.

3. Container Runtime
Runs containers.
Example: Docker, containerd, CRI‑O.


📦 4. Important Kubernetes Objects (Simple Explanation)
1. Pod
Smallest deployable unit.
One or more containers working together.

2. Deployment
Manages Pods.
Supports rolling updates and scaling.

3. ReplicaSet
Ensures a fixed number of Pod replicas are always running.

4. Service
Used for stable networking.
Types:
ClusterIP → internal communication
NodePort → expose service on each node
LoadBalancer → expose service to the internet

5. Namespace
Logical separation inside cluster.

6. ConfigMap
Stores non‑secret configuration (env vars, properties).

7. Secret
Stores sensitive data (passwords, tokens).

8. Volume / PersistentVolume / PVC
Storage system for Pods.


🎯 5. How Kubernetes Works (Simple Flow)
You submit a YAML → kubectl apply -f file.yaml
API server accepts request
Scheduler picks a node
Kubelet runs Pod on that node
Controller ensures desired state
Service exposes it for access

✔️ 6. One-Line Summary:-
Kubernetes is a powerful platform that automatically runs, manages, scales, and heals containerized applications across multiple machines.


WHAT IS A KUBERNETES CLUSTER?
A Kubernetes cluster is a group of machines (nodes) that work together to run containerized applications.

It has two parts:
1. Control Plane (Master Nodes)
   - Makes decisions about the cluster (scheduling, scaling, monitoring).

2. Worker Nodes
   - Run the actual application pods.

Cluster = Control Plane + Worker Nodes


TYPES OF KUBERNETES CLUSTERS:-

1. SELF-MANAGED KUBERNETES CLUSTER
(You install, configure, secure, and maintain everything)

Examples:
- kubeadm
- Bare-metal clusters
- Kubespray
- kops

Advantages:
- Full control over configuration
- Flexible customization
- Cost efficient on bare-metal
- Good for learning and on‑prem use cases

Disadvantages:
- Harder to set up
- You manage upgrades, security patches
- Requires strong DevOps/SRE skills

Used When:
- On-premise data centers
- Custom networking/storage needs
- Enterprises needing full control


2. CLOUD-MANAGED (MANAGED KUBERNETES) CLUSTER
(Cloud provider manages the control plane)

Examples:
- Amazon EKS
- Azure AKS
- Google GKE
- Oracle OKE
- DigitalOcean Kubernetes
- IBM Cloud Kubernetes

Advantages:
- Control plane managed by cloud (easy)
- Automatic upgrades and patches
- High availability built-in
- Integrates with cloud networking, storage, IAM

Disadvantages:
- Higher cost
- Less control over master components
- Cloud vendor lock-in

Used When:
- Production workloads
- Startups and enterprises
- When simplicity and reliability is required


SELF-MANAGED VS CLOUD-MANAGED (COMPARISON)

Setup              | Self-Managed: Manual (kubeadm/kops) | Cloud-Managed: Automatic
Control Plane      | You manage                          | Cloud provider manages
Upgrades           | Manual                               | Automatic
Cost               | Lower                                | Higher
Flexibility        | High                                 | Medium
Skill Needed       | High                                 | Low-Medium
Best Use Case      | On-prem/custom workloads             | Production/cloud-native


TYPES OF CLOUD KUBERNETES MODELS

1. Fully Managed Kubernetes
   (EKS, AKS, GKE)
   Cloud manages:
   - Control plane
   - Networking
   - Some security
   - Scaling

2. Hosted Kubernetes (Older Model)
   Examples:
   - Older GKE versions
   - Rancher managed clusters

3. Hybrid Kubernetes (On-prem + Cloud)
   Examples:
   - EKS Anywhere
   - GKE On-Prem
   - Azure Arc Kubernetes

Summary:
- Cluster = Control Plane + Worker Nodes
- Self-managed = Full control, more effort
- Cloud-managed = Easy, production-ready
- Cloud options = EKS, AKS, GKE
- Hybrid = Mix of on-prem and cloud


WHAT IS kubectl?
kubectl is the command-line tool used to interact with the Kubernetes cluster.

You use kubectl to:
- Deploy applications
- Check cluster resources
- Inspect logs
- Scale applications
- Debug issues
- Manage cluster objects (Pods, Deployments, Services, etc.)

kubectl communicates with the Kubernetes API Server.


BASIC KUBECTL COMMANDS (MOST IMPORTANT)

1. Cluster Information
----------------------
kubectl version
kubectl cluster-info
kubectl get nodes


2. List Resources
------------------
kubectl get pods
kubectl get pods -o wide
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get services
kubectl get namespaces

List everything:
kubectl get all


3. Describe Resources (Detailed Info)
--------------------------------------
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
kubectl describe deployment <deployment-name>
kubectl describe node <node-name>


4. Create / Apply YAML
------------------------
kubectl apply -f <file.yaml>
kubectl create -f <file.yaml>

Dry run (no changes made):
kubectl apply -f <file.yaml> --dry-run=client


5. Delete Resources
--------------------
kubectl delete pod <pod-name>
kubectl delete deployment <deployment-name>
kubectl delete -f <file.yaml>


6. Logs and Debugging
----------------------
View logs:
kubectl logs <pod-name>

Logs for a specific container:
kubectl logs <pod-name> -c <container-name>

Exec into pod (like SSH):
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- sh
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- bash


7. Scale and Update
----------------------
Scale deployment:
kubectl scale deployment <name> --replicas=3

Update image in deployment:
kubectl set image deployment/<name> <container-name>=<new-image>


8. Namespaces
---------------
kubectl get ns
kubectl create ns <name>

Run command in specific namespace:
kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

Change default namespace:
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=<ns>


9. Config and Context
-----------------------
kubectl config view
kubectl config get-contexts
kubectl config use-context <context-name>


10. Apply Imperative Commands (Shortcut)
-----------------------------------------
Run a pod quickly:
kubectl run mypod --image=nginx

Expose pod as service:
kubectl expose pod mypod --port=80 --type=NodePort

KUBERNETES: IMPERATIVE VS DECLARATIVE APPROACH (CLEAN NOTES)

WHAT IS IMPERATIVE APPROACH?
Imperative approach means:
“You tell Kubernetes EXACTLY what to do, step by step.”

You run kubectl commands directly to create/update/delete resources.

Examples:
- kubectl run
- kubectl create
- kubectl delete
- kubectl expose
- kubectl scale

FEATURES OF IMPERATIVE:
- Quick and easy
- Good for learning and testing
- No YAML needed
- Not ideal for production (no version control)

COMMON IMPERATIVE COMMANDS:
----------------------------------
Create a pod:
kubectl run mypod --image=nginx

Create a deployment:
kubectl create deployment mydep --image=nginx

Expose a deployment as a service:
kubectl expose deployment mydep --port=80 --type=NodePort

Scale a deployment:
kubectl scale deployment mydep --replicas=3

Delete resources:
kubectl delete pod mypod
kubectl delete deployment mydep


WHAT IS DECLARATIVE APPROACH?
Declarative approach means:
“You define the desired state in a YAML file, and Kubernetes ensures the cluster matches it.”

You use:
kubectl apply -f <file.yaml>

FEATURES OF DECLARATIVE:
- Best for production
- YAML stored in Git (GitOps)
- Version-controlled
- Reproducible deployments
- Kubernetes adjusts automatically to match the desired state

EXAMPLE DECLARATIVE YAML:
----------------------------------
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: mydep
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx

Apply YAML:
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

Update YAML and re-apply:
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

Delete using YAML:
kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml


IMPERATIVE VS DECLARATIVE (SUMMARY)

Imperative:
- Direct commands
- No YAML required
- Good for quick tasks
- Not suitable for large/production setups

Declarative:
- Uses YAML files
- Version-controlled deployments
- Best for production
- Kubernetes maintains desired state


WHEN TO USE WHAT?

Use Imperative When:
- Testing
- Learning
- Creating something quick
- Temporary resources

Use Declarative When:
- Production applications
- CI/CD pipelines
- GitOps workflows
- Teams working together
- Anything that must be tracked and versioned

KUBERNETES LABELS AND SELECTORS (CLEAN NOTES)

WHAT ARE LABELS?
Labels are key-value pairs attached to Kubernetes objects such as:
- Pods
- Deployments
- Services
- Nodes

Example:
  labels:
    app: frontend
    env: dev

Purpose:
- Organize resources
- Filter and group objects
- Select specific objects using selectors
- Enable rolling updates, autoscaling, and service discovery


LABEL SYNTAX:
- Key: must match DNS label format (e.g., app, env, tier)
- Value: free-form string

Examples:
app: myapp
env: prod
tier: backend


BASIC LABEL COMMANDS

Add a label:
kubectl label pod <pod-name> app=frontend

Update/overwrite a label:
kubectl label pod <pod-name> env=production --overwrite

Delete a label:
kubectl label pod <pod-name> env-

View labels:
kubectl get pods --show-labels

Filter using label:
kubectl get pods -l app=frontend


WHAT ARE SELECTORS?
Selectors are used to filter Kubernetes objects based on labels.

Two types:
1. Equality-based selectors:
   - app=frontend
   - env!=prod

2. Set-based selectors:
   - env in (dev,qa)
   - tier notin (cache)
   - app in (frontend,backend)


SELECTOR EXAMPLES:

Get pods with specific label:
kubectl get pods -l app=frontend

Select pods NOT having a label:
kubectl get pods -l 'env!=prod'

Select using set-based:
kubectl get pods -l 'env in (dev,qa)'
kubectl get pods -l 'tier notin (cache)'


LABELS IN YAML (EXAMPLE):

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: mypod
  labels:
    app: frontend
    env: dev
spec:
  containers:
    - name: c1
      image: nginx


WHERE SELECTORS ARE USED?

1. Deployment:
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: frontend

2. Service:
spec:
  selector:
    app: frontend

3. ReplicaSet:
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      tier: backend

Services use selectors to know:
- Which pods to send traffic to

Deployments use selectors to know:
- Which pods they control


IMPORTANT SELECTOR COMMANDS:

Get pods using multiple labels:
kubectl get pods -l 'app=frontend,env=dev'

Get deployments using selector:
kubectl get deployment -l app=frontend

Delete pods using label:
kubectl delete pod -l app=frontend

Delete any resource by label:
kubectl delete all -l app=myapp


SUMMARY:
- Labels = key-value tags on objects.
- Selectors = filters to find objects.
- Services and Deployments depend on labels/selectors.
- Set-based selectors allow advanced filtering.

KUBERNETES NODE SELECTOR (CLEAN NOTES)

WHAT IS A NODE SELECTOR?
A nodeSelector is the simplest way to schedule a Pod onto specific Nodes.
It tells Kubernetes:
“Run this Pod ONLY on nodes that have this label.”

NodeSelector uses key-value pairs of node labels.

Example:
nodeSelector:
  disktype: ssd

This means: schedule the Pod only on nodes where the node label is disktype=ssd.


HOW TO LABEL A NODE:
--------------------------------
View nodes:
kubectl get nodes

Add a label:
kubectl label node <node-name> disktype=ssd

Check node labels:
kubectl get nodes --show-labels


POD YAML WITH NODE SELECTOR:
--------------------------------
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: mypod
spec:
  nodeSelector:
    disktype: ssd
  containers:
    - name: c1
      image: nginx

This Pod will only run on nodes labeled disktype=ssd.


WHAT IF NO NODE MATCHES?
--------------------------------
If no node has the label defined in nodeSelector:
- Pod will stay in Pending state
- Scheduler cannot place it anywhere


USE CASES OF NODE SELECTOR:
--------------------------------
- Schedule workloads on high-performance nodes
- Assign certain apps to SSD-based nodes
- Separate dev / prod workloads
- GPU nodes for ML workloads
- Node dedicated for logging, monitoring, etc.


LIMITATIONS:
--------------------------------
- Very basic scheduling method
- Exact match only (key=value)
- No advanced rules (use affinity for complex scheduling)

For advanced scheduling use:
- nodeAffinity
- taints & tolerations


SUMMARY:
--------------------------------
- nodeSelector binds a Pod to nodes with matching labels
- You must label nodes manually
- Simple and effective for basic scheduling

KUBERNETES SERVICES – NOTES
===========================

Kubernetes Services provide stable networking and consistent access to Pods.
Pods are temporary and their IPs change frequently, so Services give a fixed
endpoint to reach applications.

Kubernetes has 3 main Service types:
1. ClusterIP
2. NodePort
3. LoadBalancer

------------------------------------------------------------
1. CLUSTERIP SERVICE
------------------------------------------------------------
• Default Service type in Kubernetes.
• Exposes the application ONLY inside the cluster.
• Not accessible from outside the cluster.
• Used for internal communication between Pods or microservices.
• Provides a stable internal IP.

Use Cases:
– Backend services
– Database services
– Internal APIs

Why ClusterIP?
– Pods have dynamic IPs; ClusterIP gives a permanent internal endpoint.

Example:
type: ClusterIP

------------------------------------------------------------
2. NODEPORT SERVICE
------------------------------------------------------------
• Exposes the application externally using <NodeIP>:<NodePort>.
• NodePort range: 30000–32767.
• All Nodes expose the same port, making it reachable externally.
• Used for testing or small environments without a LoadBalancer.

Use Cases:
– Access app from outside without Ingress
– Development and testing

Why NodePort?
– Provides simple external access without cloud dependencies.

Example:
type: NodePort
nodePort: 30080

------------------------------------------------------------
3. LOADBALANCER SERVICE
------------------------------------------------------------
• Exposes the application to the internet with a PUBLIC IP.
• Cloud provider (AWS, Azure, GCP) creates a load balancer.
• Distributes traffic across multiple Nodes and Pods.
• Best for production-grade services.

Use Cases:
– Web applications
– Public APIs
– Production workloads

Why LoadBalancer?
– Provides external access + traffic balancing + high availability.

Example:
type: LoadBalancer

------------------------------------------------------------
SUMMARY / COMPARISON
------------------------------------------------------------
ClusterIP:
- Internal-only
- Default service
- For communication inside the cluster

NodePort:
- Accessible using <NodeIP>:NodePort
- Simple external access
- Good for testing environments

LoadBalancer:
- Provides public IP and cloud load balancer
- Best for production
- Distributes traffic to Nodes and Pods

------------------------------------------------------------
WHY WE NEED SERVICES IN KUBERNETES
------------------------------------------------------------
• Pods are temporary; their IPs change.
• Services give a stable, permanent network endpoint.
• Enable internal and external access.
• Provide load balancing across multiple Pods.
• Enable service discovery using DNS names instead of IP addresses.

BASIC CODE:-
---------------------------------
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: service-name
spec:
  type: ServiceType     # ClusterIP / NodePort / LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: app-label      # Match with pod labels
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80          # Service Port
      targetPort: 8080  # Pod/container port
      # nodePort: 30080 # Only for NodePort or LoadBalancer

to add state file to s3:-
export KOPS_STATE_STORE=s3://<Bucket_name>

